      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Surgery of lumbar disc herniation is one of the most common neurosurgical procedures. Since the introduction of the microscope, the so-called microscopic discectomy (MD) has represented the \"gold standard\" technique until today. Here, the authors present clinical outcome of patients treated for of lumbar disc herniation with a mean follow-up of more than 30 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A retrospective outcome study enrolled 85 patients who underwent a 1- to 2-level MD or standard open discectomy (SOD) 27 to 40 years ago. The pre- and postoperative clinical status and the surgical record were analyzed for all patients. A standardized questionnaire which included the current neurological status, EQ-5D, PSI, Odom criteria, Oswestry Disability Index (ODI), and limitations in quality of life because of the operation was answered by each patient."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The mean follow-up was 32.1 years, 50 patients were operated on via MD, and 35 via SOD. Preoperatively, the mean pain intensity for leg pain was 9 on the Numeric Pain Rating Scale (NPRS), and 1 at follow-up. Median EQ-5D was 6, according to PSI 93% of the patients were satisfied, according to Odom criteria 91% of the patients reported good-to-excellent functional recovery. The mean ODI was 15% (range 0-60%). Eight patients had a second surgery because of recurrent disc herniation, 13 patients had a second surgery because of a new disc herniation at the adjacent level. Two patients underwent a third procedure. The recurrent herniation rate was 8% in the MD group and 12% in SOD group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The long-term follow-up data of this study reveals excellent clinical results even after more than 30 years. Overall rate for second surgery was 25%. About 15% of all patients needed a second surgery because of a disc herniation at the adjacent segment. No difference in long-term clinical outcome could be demonstrated for microdiscectomy and open discectomy."
        }
      ]},
